{"id": "365ef8da-459e-40ca-94a4-b4639ab99cd7", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T19:01:56.976435", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "35844574-9146-4ead-84e6-c78f8f41a68a", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-e5aab908-2adc-4ab2-ae0c-36e1d5902136", "tool_call": {"id": "call-e5aab908-2adc-4ab2-ae0c-36e1d5902136", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Submit initial plan-stage handoff for ZB-2p bubble-rate design\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Stage=plan — initial inspection, approach outline, and open questions\\n\\nThis is a design-only task. Below is (1) what is visibly available in the workspace, (2) a preliminary approach idea for measuring ZB-2p's \\\"near-zero\\\" bubble claim, and (3) the assumptions that the approach leans on plus the uncertainties I would resolve with the researcher before turning this into a full design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1) Read-only inspection of /workspace and inputs\\n\\nWhat I actually found:\\n\\n- `/workspace/task.json` — the only file in the workspace. Its `documents.track.md` literal text is: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"*\\n- No source trees: no Megatron-LM fork, no DeepSpeed, no Zero-Bubble-Pipeline repo, no notebooks, no docs.\\n- No datasets, no checkpoints, no profiler trace files.\\n- Python 3.12.3 is present, but `pip list` shows no `torch`, `deepspeed`, `apex`, or `transformers`; only system packages.\\n- `nvidia-smi` binary exists at `/usr/bin/nvidia-smi` but `NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver`, consistent with `environment.gpu_enabled: false`.\\n- 24 logical CPUs, network disabled, no `/home/actor` content beyond an empty home directory.\\n- No `track.md` file materialized anywhere on disk — the reference inside `task.json` appears to be a note in the task description rather than a reachable document.\\n\\nInformation that I cannot verify from inside the workspace (would need researcher confirmation or external lookup):\\n\\n- The exact upstream implementation of ZB-2p being targeted. The publicly known home of the algorithm is the `sail-sg/zero-bubble-pipeline` repo (paper \\\"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\\\", ICLR 2024, Qi et al.) and a later Megatron-LM integration that exposes `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward`.\\n- The canonical activation rule: setting `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 × num_pipeline_stages` is the only --flag-named clue given in the task prompt. The paper's ZB-H1 uses `num_stages`; ZB-2p uses `2 × num_stages`. I am treating this mapping as the working hypothesis, not as confirmed for the specific codebase the researcher has in mind.\\n- No profile traces, model definitions, or micro-batch / global-batch / pipeline-stage specifications are available; these will have to come from the researcher.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2) Preliminary approach outline (not a full design yet)\\n\\nGoal: test, on multi-GPU hardware, whether the schedule produced by the ZB-2p scheduler has a measured bubble fraction close to zero.\\n\\nCore idea in one sentence: instrument either the scheduler itself (counting idle \\\"no-op\\\" micro-steps per stage per iteration) or the device profile stream (CUDA gap spots on each rank), then express bubble fraction as idle time over steady-state iteration time, and report this for ZB-2p alongside non-zero-bubble baselines across enough micro-batches per pipeline that steady state dominates warmup.\\n\\nHypotheses to test:\\n\\n- H1 (positive): with global micro-batches-per-pipeline ≫ 1, the bubble fraction under ZB-2p converges to ≈ 0 within the measurement floor of the chosen instrumentation.\\n- H2 (negative / partial): the measured bubble fraction is dominated by cross-stage P2P communication and intra-stage recompute, not by pure scheduling stall, so an \\\"ideal bubble\\\" definition looks near-zero while an \\\"all stalls\\\" definition does not.\\n- H3 (boundary): ZB-2p's \\\"near zero\\\" property holds only inside the regime the paper analyzed — homogeneous per-stage compute, fully synchronous P2P, and parameter-microbatch counts above some threshold; outside that regime the schedule degrades.\\n\\nKey variables to vary across runs:\\n\\n- # pipeline stages `P` (e.g., 2, 4, 8).\\n- # micro-batches per pipeline `M` (e.g., 2P, 4P, 8P, where \\\"large\\\" pushes the schedule toward its asymptotic regime).\\n- Model shape proxy (GPT-like stack depth/width or a simpler MLP-only model, since theory is shape-agnostic but recompute / activation memory bound is not).\\n- Schedule variant as a categorical factor: 1F1B (baseline non-zero-bubble), Interleaved 1F1B (baseline with smaller theoretical bubble), ZB-H1, ZB-2p.\\n\\nTwo complementary measurement angles (each captures a different definition of \\\"bubble\\\"):\\n\\n- Scheduler-intrinsic definition: count, per stage, the number of micro-steps whose computed executable set was empty. Bubble fraction = sum(empty steps across stages) / (P × total steps). Pros: matches the paper's definition; not sensitive to communication. Cons: requires the scheduler's event log.\\n- Wall-clock definition: per stage, bubble = (iteration time − maximal busy interval across stages − communication time). Bubble fraction = bubble / iteration time. Pros: matches what a user actually feels. Cons: requires attributing overlap time correctly between compute, P2P, and NCCL collectives.\\n\\nHow groups and controls could be set up:\\n\\n- Treatment group: ZB-2p (`--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 × P`).\\n- Control groups: 1F1B; Interleaved 1F1B (with its virtual-stage factor `v`); ZB-H1 (`--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = P`) as the closest non-2p variant.\\n- A within-run reference for \\\"true zero\\\": for the same shape, compute the scheduler-reported ideal bubble fraction at runtime and compare it to the measured one; both should fall under the same instrumentation floor on healthy runs.\\n\\nDefinition of \\\"near zero\\\" to be adopted in the final design (pending confirmation): bubble fraction is \\\"near zero\\\" if (a) the scheduler-intrinsic bubble is ≤ a small constant independent of `M` for `M ≳ 4P`, AND (b) the wall-clock bubble − ideal bubble is bounded by the measured P2P / recompute floor of the run. Either condition alone would not be conclusive.\\n\\nWhat multi-GPU profiling arrangement requires explicit decision (cannot be resolved from the workspace):\\n\\n- Per-rank `torch.cuda.synchronize()` at iteration boundaries vs. CUDA event pair bracketing each micro-step; CUPTI / `torch.profiler` timeline vs. custom NVTX ranges.\\n- Whether warmup iterations are excluded from the bubble bookkeeping and how many warmup iters are needed for the run to reach periodic steady state.\\n- Whether weights / optimizer step latency counts as bubble (ZB-2p allows overlap of W with subsequent F, so this is a real question), whether separate loss / log / checkpoint steps fall inside the measured interval, and whether activation recomputation for the backward pass is on.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3) Key assumptions and unresolved uncertainties\\n\\nAssumptions I am making until told otherwise:\\n\\n- A1. The ZB-2p scheduler is available in whatever codebase the researcher is targeting (most likely either `sail-sg/zero-bubble-pipeline` or NVIDIA's Megatron-LM fork with the ZB integration), and the activation rule `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 × num_pipeline_stages` is the correct switch for that codebase. *Cannot be confirmed from the workspace.*\\n- A2. \\\"Near-zero bubble rate\\\" is meant in the steady-state, large-`M` regime — i.e., transient / warmup / cooldown bubbles are not what the question is about.\\n- A3. \\\"Multiple GPUs\\\" means a homogeneously connected pool (same bandwidth across neighboring stages), and the model + micro-batch is sized such that no stage is memory- or activation-swapping-bottlenecked.\\n- A4. P2P latency and NCCL collectives are available and measurable; activation checkpointing (recompute) is either off or its cost can be subtracted.\\n- A5. \\\"Bubble\\\" excludes optimizer state I/O, logging, and gradient all-reduce outside the pipeline — i.e., the comparison is between schedule variants on identical infrastructure, not against an absolute lower bound.\\n\\nUncertainties I want the researcher's input on before finalizing the design:\\n\\n- U1. Which codebase / fork / version of ZB-2p is the target? The pipeline timing instrumentation differs significantly between the standalone implementation and the Megatron-LM integration.\\n- U2. Which model and which `P` and `M` are in scope? Without those, I cannot bound how many iterations are needed for steady state or whether GPU memory will force recompute on.\\n- U3. Definition of bubble: scheduler-ideal (paper-style) or wall-clock-inclusive-of-communication-and-recompute? The answer changes what counts as \\\"near zero\\\" by orders of magnitude.\\n- U4. Warmup policy: how many warmup micro-batches / iterations to discard, and whether the warmup itself contains the largest bubble fraction we should be allowed to ignore.\\n- U5. Comparison set: must 1F1B and/or Interleaved 1F1B and/or ZB-H1 all be present as controls, or is ZB-2p vs. an analytical lower bound enough? The paper compares against 1F1B and ZB-H1, not Interleaved 1F1B in its headline numbers, but Interleaved 1F1B is the more competitive conventional baseline.\\n- U6. Communication accounting: should we report bubble with P2P time charged to bubble, or report bubble assuming communication is fully overlapped with compute? Different papers do different things.\\n- U7. Tolerance for \\\"near zero\\\": is a measured residual bubble < some absolute epsilon (e.g., < 1% of steady-state time) sufficient, or is the standard what's needed (no measurable bubble above the instrument's quantization floor)?\\n- U8. Number of runs and statistical treatment: per `(P, M, schedule)` cell, how many repeats, and are we comparing distributions or point estimates? Memory contention between co-located runs on shared GPU nodes can add noise that has to be controlled for or randomized away.\\n- U9. The GPU environment for measurement: is profiling on the same node(s) that the schedule will run, with CUDA event / CUPTI / NVTX allowed, or are we constrained to higher-level wall-clock timing?\\n\\nI will convert this into a full experimental design (stage=result) once the researcher confirms U1–U9 (or at least the subset they want to lock down) and confirms A1–A5.\",\"evidence\":\"Inspection findings carried into evidence:\\n\\n- `find /workspace -type f` returned only `/workspace/task.json`. Verified by re-running `ls -la /workspace`.\\n- `task.json.documents.track.md` is the literal string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" (read directly from /workspace/task.json).\\n- `nvidia-smi` is present at `/usr/bin/nvidia-smi` but the binary fails with \\\"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver,\\\" matching `environment.gpu_enabled: false`.\\n- `pip3 list` shows system packages only; no `torch` / `deepspeed` / `apex` / `transformers`; Python is 3.12.3; 24 logical CPUs; `network: disabled` per `task.json.environment`.\\n- No `track.md` file exists anywhere under `/` (depth-5 `find` returned no rows).\\n- Claims about ZB-2p's flag, definition, and theoretical regime are sourced from the publicly known \\\"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\\\" paper (Qi et al., ICLR 2024) and its reference implementation; treated as working hypotheses to confirm with the researcher, not as in-repo facts.\\n- No executed experiments were performed and no measurements or numerical outcomes are reported.\",\"assumptions\":\"See the A1–A5 block in section 3 of the answer; restated here:\\n\\n- A1. ZB-2p is reachable via `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 × num_pipeline_stages` in some codebase the researcher has in scope; this was not verifiable from the empty workspace.\\n- A2. \\\"Near-zero bubble rate\\\" is assessed in steady state, not including warmup / cooldown.\\n- A3. The multi-GPU setup is homogeneous and not memory/activation-bottlenecked.\\n- A4. P2P and NCCL are available and measurable; recompute is either off or its cost can be separated.\\n- A5. \\\"Bubble\\\" excludes optimizer I/O, logging, and gradient all-reduce outside the pipeline.\",\"uncertainties\":\"See the U1–U9 block in section 3 of the answer; restated here:\\n\\n- U1. Which codebase/version of ZB-2p is the target.\\n- U2. Which model shape, P (pipeline stages), and M (micro-batches per pipeline) are in scope.\\n- U3. Bubble definition: scheduler-ideal vs. wall-clock-inclusive of communication and recompute.\\n- U4. Warmup policy and number of iterations to discard.\\n- U5. Required control schedules (1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B, ZB-H1, analytical bound).\\n- U6. Communication accounting: charge P2P to bubble vs. assume overlap.\\n- U7. \\\"Near zero\\\" tolerance: absolute fraction vs. instrumentation floor.\\n- U8. Number of repeats per cell and statistical comparison.\\n- U9. Profiler constraints: CUDA events / CUPTI / NVTX vs. wall-clock only.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-d399e7c7-73b2-48cb-8341-c5b1b8b93c02", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
