{"id": "500b6395-8964-4ea7-8588-db56c694c160", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T19:03:22.337308", "source": "user", "parent_id": "f553780a-222f-46f5-9eda-3eec883ef79f", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved, with the following binding decisions to remove ambiguity in the final design. Please incorporate them directly and do not re-open these points unless you find a contradiction with the source material you can cite. Items not addressed below should follow the defaults you proposed in your plan, unless noted otherwise. Do not run any experiments or invent measurement values; the deliverable remains a design document with rationale and feasibility analysis. Locked decisions and guidance: U1 (codebase). Target the ZB-2p integration in the Megatron-LM-style flag surface described in the task prompt (i.e., --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 x num_pipeline_stages is the enabling switch). Treat the profiling hooks as if both (a) a per-stage scheduler event log (empty executable-set events) and (b) a CUDA-event / NVTX timeline on each rank are available; describe both and note which is primary for the scheduler-ideal metric and which is primary for the wall-clock metric. U2 (model shape and regime). Default to a GPT-like Transformer block (standard for the paper's experiments) and require the design to define (P, M) cells covering at least: P in {2, 4, 8} and M/P in {2, 4, 8}, so steady state dominates and large-M asymptotic behaviour is reachable. U3 (bubble definition). Adopt both definitions explicitly and compute both per cell: (i) scheduler-ideal bubble fraction = sum of empty executable-set micro-steps across all stages divided by (P x total micro-steps in steady state); (ii) wall-clock bubble fraction = (iteration time - maximal busy interval across stages - P2P/NCCL communication time accounted as overlap) divided by iteration time. State both and reason about the gap between them. U4 (warmup). Discard the first 2 x P iterations per cell, and additionally discard the final 1 iteration; report whether a periodic-steady-state sanity check (e.g., per-stage busy-interval variance across iterations) holds before the kept window. U5 (controls). Run all four schedules as cells of a single full-factorial design where feasible: 1F1B (the paper's primary baseline), Interleaved 1F1B with virtual-stage factor v in {1, 2} (the strongest conventional baseline), ZB-H1 (--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = P, the closest non-2p zero-bubble variant), and ZB-2p (treatment). State up-front which comparisons are primary (ZB-2p vs. 1F1B, ZB-2p vs. ZB-H1) and which are secondary (vs. Interleaved 1F1B). U6 (communication accounting). Do not charge P2P/NCCL to bubble; instead treat communication as a separately reported cost and report the overlap ratio between communication and compute on each stage. Any residual idle time that is neither compute nor accounted-for communication is reported as wall-clock bubble. U7 (near-zero tolerance). Define pass criteria in advance: (a) scheduler-ideal bubble fraction must be 0 by construction for ZB-2p in the kept window and this must be verifiable from the scheduler log; (b) wall-clock bubble fraction must be at or below the run's measured communication/compute floor (i.e., bubble within profiler resolution). If the scheduler-ideal is non-zero or the wall-clock bubble exceeds the floor by more than a stated epsilon, the claim is not supported for that cell. U8 (replication). Per cell, plan N >= 5 timed iterations under fixed seed; report median and IQR of bubble fraction; randomize cell order across runs to avoid time-correlated noise. U9 (profiler). Allow CUDA events and NVTX ranges; declare the achievable measurement floor in absolute microseconds per micro-step as part of the design and show how it bounds (b) above. In addition to the locked decisions, please address two items in the final design: (F1) a feasibility and risk section that lists what can break the ZB-2p near-zero claim in practice (e.g., load imbalance between stages, heterogeneous interconnect, recompute interaction, optimizer/weight-update overlap policy in the chosen codebase, memory-pressure-driven stalls) and for each risk states the corresponding mitigation or disqualifier; (F2) a reproducibility section specifying the exact prompt-style flags (including --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward, pipeline-model-parallel-size, and any others you judge necessary), the cell list, the data to record per cell (raw per-iteration busy intervals, idle events, communication timings, overlap ratios), and the analysis script plan that turns those records into the two bubble-fraction estimates. Proceed to produce the final design.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 26}
{"id": "5fa6643a-f112-483b-bf22-de87354e4a19", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T19:05:21.473679", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "500b6395-8964-4ea7-8588-db56c694c160", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experimental Design: Does ZB-2p Achieve Near-Zero Bubble Rate on Multi-GPU?\n\nDesign-only document. Source-supported setup from the user's locked decisions (U1–U9 + F1, F2) is used directly. Where the design hinges on facts about the ZB-2p scheduler itself, those facts are tied to the publicly known \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\" paper (Qi, Yi, You, ICLR 2024) and its reference Megatron-LM-style integration; they are treated as the working source of truth until the specific codebase SHA is fixed.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Scope and question\n\nTest, on a multi-GPU pool, whether the schedule emitted by the ZB-2p scheduler (Megatron-LM-style flag surface, enabled by `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 × num_pipeline_stages`) has a measured bubble fraction close to zero when run under steady-state conditions on a GPT-like Transformer block.\n\nThe design produces two numeric estimates per `(P, M, schedule)` cell: a **scheduler-ideal** bubble fraction and a **wall-clock** bubble fraction. The pass rule (Section 6) requires both to fall under stated thresholds.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Locked-design summary (binding from the researcher)\n\n| ID | Decision | Adopted as |\n|----|----------|-----------|\n| U1 | Codebase = Megatron-LM-style ZB-2p; profiling uses both scheduler event log and CUDA-event/NVTX timeline | Both instruments are described; (a) is primary for scheduler-ideal metric, (b) is primary for wall-clock metric |\n| U2 | Model = GPT-like Transformer; cells cover P ∈ {2, 4, 8} × M/P ∈ {2, 4, 8} | Full 3 × 3 cell grid per schedule |\n| U3 | Two definitions are computed per cell: scheduler-ideal and wall-clock | Both computed; gap is reported and reasoned about |\n| U4 | Discard first 2 × P iterations and the final iteration; per-stage busy-interval variance is checked before the kept window | Strict inclusion rule; steady-state sanity check is a precondition, not a result |\n| U5 | Schedules: 1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B v ∈ {1, 2}, ZB-H1, ZB-2p | Four schedule families; v is a within-Interleaved-1F1B factor, not a cross-schedule factor |\n| U6 | P2P/NCCL is not charged to bubble; comm is reported separately with an overlap ratio | Wall-clock bubble = idle time not attributable to compute or measured comm |\n| U7 | Pass criteria: (a) scheduler-ideal = 0 by construction and verifiable from the log; (b) wall-clock bubble ≤ measured comm/compute floor + ε | Pre-stated; failure on either criterion fails the cell |\n| U8 | N ≥ 5 timed iterations per cell; report median + IQR; randomized cell order | Fixed before any launch |\n| U9 | CUDA events + NVTX allowed; absolute μs floor per micro-step is declared in advance | Floor is measured empirically on the target hardware before the design is considered live |\n\nSection 7 (F1) lists feasiblity / risk and the disqualifiers each implies.\nSection 8 (F2) is the reproducibility plan (flags, cell list, data records, analysis script).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Definitions used in this document\n\n- **P**: pipeline-model-parallel size (number of stages).\n- **M**: number of micro-batches per pipeline (i.e., `gradient_accumulation_steps` set such that global_batch = `P × M × micro_batch_size`).\n- **v**: Interleaved 1F1B virtual-stage factor. v = 1 collapses to a vanilla 1F1B layout (no stage-chunking) and is reported alongside the explicit 1F1B cell to make the equivalence checkable.\n- **μ-step**: one ranked, type-tagged operation (`F`, `B`, `W`, `recv`, `send`) emitted by the scheduler for one (stage, micro-batch).\n- **Steady-state window**: per cell, iterations `[2P, T-1]` where T is the last iteration (so first 2P are discarded and the final iteration is discarded).\n- **Scheduler-ideal bubble fraction** `B_ideal`: per iteration, per stage, the number of μ-steps where the scheduler's executable-set was empty, summed over all stages, divided by `P × M`.\n- **Wall-clock bubble fraction** `B_wall`: `1 − (T_busy − T_comm,overlapped) / T_iter`, where `T_busy` is the maximal busy interval across stages (computed as the union of CUDA-event-marked compute intervals on that rank), `T_comm,overlapped` is the sub-interval of P2P / NCCL time that overlaps with compute on the same rank, and `T_iter` is the per-rank iteration wall time.\n- **Overlap ratio** `ρ_comm`: `T_comm,overlapped / T_comm` per rank; reported separately, not folded into `B_wall`.\n\nThe two metrics are linked by:\n`B_wall ≥ B_ideal + ε(floor)`\nwhere `ε(floor)` is the gap attributable to whatever the scheduler accounts for as \"busy\" but the CUDA-event view sees as idle (e.g., plus small synchronization margins below the profiler floor); below the profiler floor the gap is unmeasurable.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Cell design\n\n### 4.1 Cell grid\n\nFor each schedule in `{1F1B, Interleaved v=1, Interleaved v=2, ZB-H1, ZB-2p}`, the cell grid is:\n\n| | M/P = 2 | M/P = 4 | M/P = 8 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P = 2 | M = 4 | M = 8 | M = 16 |\n| P = 4 | M = 8 | M = 16 | M = 32 |\n| P = 8 | M = 16 | M = 32 | M = 64 |\n\nCell count: 5 schedule families × 9 (P, M) cells = **45 cells**. Each cell is repeated N ≥ 5 times = **≥ 225 timed runs** (and per run, ~2P + N iteration-level records after warmup).\n\n### 4.2 Primary vs. secondary comparisons (declared in advance, per U5)\n\n- **Primary (decision-driving):**\n  - ZB-2p vs. 1F1B, on every (P, M) cell.\n  - ZB-2p vs. ZB-H1, on every (P, M) cell.\n- **Secondary (contextual):**\n  - ZB-2p vs. Interleaved 1F1B (v = 2), on every (P, M) cell.\n  - Interleaved v = 1 vs. vanilla 1F1B equivalence check (sanity, not part of pass/fail).\n\nThe Interleaved v = 1 ⟺ vanilla 1F1B equivalence cell acts as a control: if the two diverge by more than the floor, the cell is suspicious and excluded from the headline numbers with a recorded rationale.\n\n### 4.3 What \"warmup\" and \"steady state\" mean concretely\n\n- **Iteration budget per run**: `T = 2P + 1 + N` (the first 2P are warmup, the last 1 is dropped, the middle N are timed). For the largest cell (P = 8), `T = 22`; N = 5 timed iterations dominate, so steady state is required to hold inside those 5 iterations.\n- **Steady-state check (precondition to keep the cell)**: for the N timed iterations, compute the per-stage busy-interval length in each iteration; the cell passes the precondition if the **median absolute deviation across the N repetitions** for every stage's median busy interval is below a per-cell threshold (default proposal: ≤ 5% of the cell's iteration time). If this fails, the cell is re-run under the diagnostic mode (Section 6.2) before being declared failed or passed.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Instrumentation plan\n\nTwo independent instruments, one per metric:\n\n### 5.1 Scheduler event log → scheduler-ideal bubble `B_ideal`\n\n- **Primary hook**: the scheduler's per-stage executable-set state at the end of each μ-step. Each emitted event is `{stage, μ-step index, micro-batch index, op ∈ {F, B, W}, exec_set_size_at_dispatch, ts_dispatch, ts_complete}`.\n- **Empty-set counter**: `B_ideal` is computed as\n  `Σ over (stage, μ-step) of 1[exec_set_size_at_dispatch == 0] / (P × M)`\n  inside the steady-state window.\n- **Why primary**: this is the metric whose zero-ness the paper (and the user's locked decision U7a) defines \"near-zero\" by. If this is non-zero for a ZB-2p cell, ZB-2p's claim is falsified for that cell regardless of wall-clock.\n- **Source assumption (paper-anchored)**: ZB-2p's schedule is constructed so that, in any iteration with the schedule in its periodic regime, every stage has at least one executable μ-step. The schedulability invariant is therefore checkable from the log.\n\n### 5.2 CUDA-event + NVTX timeline → wall-clock bubble `B_wall` and overlap `ρ_comm`\n\n- **Primary hook**: per-rank `cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)` pairs bracketing every emitted μ-step; NVTX ranges named with the same `{stage, μ-step, op}` tags so the CUDA events can be reconciled with the scheduler log.\n- **Boundary sync**: one `cuda.synchronize()` at the start of iteration and one at the end of iteration; intra-iteration timing uses event pairs without a global sync, so μ-step resolution is not gated by ring-buffer depth.\n- **Compute intervals**: begin = event-start of a `F/B/W` μ-step dispatch; end = event-complete of the same op. Multiple ops on the same stage may chain into a single contiguous interval if they share a CUDA stream and there is no intervening host-side wait — the merge rule is precise (no overlap on the same stream) and produces a noise-free union.\n- **Communication intervals**: tagged via NVTX around `torch.distributed.send`/`recv` and around any NCCL collective boundary. Per-rank P2P / NCCL time = sum of those interval durations on that rank.\n- **Overlap**: `T_comm,overlapped` on a rank = length of `interval_comm ∩ interval_busy` for that rank, summed.\n- **Why primary for `B_wall`**: it is the only way to distinguish compute from comm from idle on the same rank, which is required by U6.\n\n### 5.3 Profiler floor declaration (U9)\n\n- **Floor measurement (design-time, not run-time)**: a synthetic per-μ-step loop of `n = 1000` event-pair.create / record / synchronize / elapsed_time sequences is run on the target hardware. The 99th-percentile `cudaEventElapsedTime` over `n` is declared as the **floor `f` in microseconds per μ-step**.\n- **Bound on `B_wall` resolution**: a single μ-step that is \"actually bubbling\" but shorter than `f` is unprovable. Therefore `B_wall` per cell has resolution `≈ P · M · f / T_iter`. This is the relevant ε used in U7b: pass requires `B_wall ≤ f_bounded` where `f_bounded = clamp(resolution × ceiling, profiler_resolution_min, stated_eps)`. A default stated ε for the design = max(resolution, 0.5%) per cell is used unless a tighter target is pre-set.\n\nThe floor `f` is recorded in the cell's metadata and reported with each cell's pass/fail decision; it is not a number being invented for this design.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Pass / fail criteria (declared a priori)\n\n### 6.1 Pass rule per cell\n\nA single `(P, M, schedule)` cell **passes** iff both:\n\n(a) **Scheduler-ideal** `B_ideal == 0` exactly, inside the steady-state window, and this is verifiable from the scheduler event log. For ZB-2p this is expected to hold by construction; for 1F1B and Interleaved 1F1B (v = 2), the expected positive value of `B_ideal` is not what is being tested here, but the same instrument must report it consistently with theory (sanity check, see 6.3).\n\n(b) **Wall-clock** `B_wall ≤ f_bounded` where `f_bounded` is the per-cell floor bound defined in Section 5.3. Equivalently, the residual idle time that is neither compute nor accounted-for communication is below the profiler's resolution.\n\nThe cell **fails** if either (a) or (b) is violated within the kept iterations, after the steady-state precondition has held.\n\n### 6.2 Diagnostic mode (when a cell fails precondition)\n\nCells whose per-stage busy-interval MAD exceeds the threshold enter a diagnostic mode that:\n1. Re-runs the cell with one-iteration resolution NVTX ranges around every μ-step and outputs the per-iteration timeline as a separate artifact (without altering the kept / dropped iteration policy).\n2. Records the run as \"diagnostic\" in the cell's metadata and excludes its summary number from the headline table.\n3. Does not silently change the pass criterion.\n\n### 6.3 Theory sanity (run once, before pass/fail is interpreted)\n\nRun the analyzer on 1F1B cells first; verify:\n- `B_ideal(1F1B) ≈ (P - 1) / (M + P - 1)` to within `±1/(P·M)` (paper expectation).\n- `B_ideal(ZB-H1) ≈ (P - 1) / (M + P - 1) · (1 - some constant)` smaller than 1F1B but still positive (paper expectation).\n- `B_ideal(Interleaved v=2) ≈ …` smaller still.\n\nIf any of these deviate by more than the per-cell resolution, the design's metric plumbing is broken and the run is treated as dataset-level failure: do not interpret ZB-2p results until the baseline instruments reconcile with theory.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility and risk analysis (F1)\n\nEach risk below: **trigger**, **consequence for the claim**, **mitigation**, **disqualifier**.\n\n### 7.1 Stage load imbalance\n- **Trigger**: per-stage busy time on a 1F1B control cell has max/stdev ratio above a stated threshold (default proposal: max / median > 1.10).\n- **Consequence**: ZB-2p's \"near zero\" claim is stage-symmetric by construction; if stages are not balanced in floating-point or activation memory, the bubble is dominated by imbalance, not by scheduling.\n- **Mitigation**: equal per-stage layer counts; vocabulary and sequence-length-specific stages (input embed, loss head) moved to a separate \"edge\" rank outside the pipeline timing window, or sized to match the body stage.\n- **Disqualifier**: any cell where stage imbalance exceeds the threshold AND the imbalance is correlated with N ≠ stable → drop the cell and record.\n\n### 7.2 Heterogeneous interconnect\n- **Trigger**: stages span nodes with non-uniform bandwidth (e.g., partial intra-rail vs cross-rail), or NVLink absent in some peer pairs.\n- **Consequence**: per-stage communication time differs; the wall-clock bubble becomes communication-bound, not scheduling-bound. ZB-2p's claim is about *scheduling* bubble, not comm.\n- **Mitigation**: restrict runs to a single fully-connected GPU island (single node, same NVLink/NVSwitch fabric between every peer pair). All stages within the same physical node.\n- **Disqualifier**: if a multi-node setup is the only option, the DDR / IB fabric differs between stage pairs → ZB-2p cells cannot be passed on `B_wall` in that environment; only `B_ideal` is reported, with a noted caveat that `B_wall` cannot be bounded.\n\n### 7.3 Activation recompute interaction\n- **Trigger**: `--recompute-activations` enabled (full or selective).\n- **Consequence**: recompute blends with the B phase; ZB-2p's split (F-before-attn / F-after-attn, B / W) is benchmarked on the *original* partition in the public reference; if recompute reorders what is sent between stages, the schedule's prefetch / overlap assumption is broken.\n- **Mitigation**: keep `--recompute-activations false` (or its ZB-aware variant, if the codebase exposes one) for the primary pass criterion. Report a separate robustness check with compute-on rather than mixing into pass/fail.\n- **Disqualifier**: if the target workload's memory budget forces recompute always-on, the design's primary pass criterion cannot be evaluated without the codebase's ZB-recompute handling, which must then be cited explicitly.\n\n### 7.4 Optimizer-step / weight-update overlap policy\n- **Trigger**: chosen codebase's ZB-2p path may or may not overlap `W` with the next iteration's `F`; this is implementation policy, not algorithm definition.\n- **Consequence**: if W is forced to be synchronous and to precede the next F, ZB-2p's \"near zero\" claim is partially defeated: the residual is now sized by `T_W / T_iter`, not by 0.\n- **Mitigation**: confirm in the target codebase that the ZB-2p schedule allows `W` to overlap with the next iteration's `F`; if not, this is a *codebase* bug or feature gap, not an algorithm failure, and is a documented disqualifier for that codebase.\n- **Disqualifier**: any codebase where W is serialized before the next F cannot pass the (a) zeroness claim for the wall-clock metric, only the scheduler-ideal metric.\n\n### 7.5 Memory pressure / allocator stalls\n- **Trigger**: an activation memory budget per stage that approaches the device's available memory, especially with large M.\n- **Consequence**: allocator-induced stalls are neither F, B, W, nor communication, so they fall into the residual (U6 \"neither compute nor accounted-for communication\"). If large enough, they push `B_wall` above the floor and the cell *looks* like bubble when in fact it is memory pressure.\n- **Mitigation**: precompute per-stage activation-memory budget `cost(P, M, model_shape)` and a `safety_margin`; reject any cell whose peak budget + optimizer state + gradient + activations exceeds device memory by less than 25 % headroom.\n- **Disqualifier**: any cell whose runtime OOM or near-OOM event is detected (logged as allocator warnings or `cudaGetLastError` nonzero) is dropped from the headline numbers.\n\n### 7.6 Profiler injection overhead\n- **Trigger**: NVTX wrapping every μ-step changes the steady-state timing.\n- **Consequence**: the *instrumented* run is the run whose bubble is being measured; injecting a perturbing instrument is a delta-t measurement not a true measurement.\n- **Mitigation**: run a non-instrumented baseline once per cell to compute `T_iter` for the denominator of `B_wall`, and run instrumented runs only for the per-μ-step interval breakdown. The bubble fraction's denominator is the non-instrumented iteration time.\n- **Disqualifier**: if the delta between instrumented and non-instrumented `T_iter` exceeds the profiler floor `f`, the cell's `B_wall` is reported as `unresolved` and excluded from the pass / fail rule.\n\n### 7.7 Cell count feasibility\n- **Trigger**: 45 cells × N ≥ 5 timed iterations × (2P + N) iterations per run.\n- **Consequence**: feasible on a multi-GPU pool if each cell fits inside ~1 GPU-hour. The largest cell (P = 8, M/P = 8) is the load-bearing run and the one most likely to be constrained by allocator, not by time.\n- **Mitigation**: randomize cell execution order across the available time budget; if a cell fails because the budget is exhausted, it is recorded as `not run` and not as `failed`.\n- **Disqualifier**: a cell whose run concludes without passing the steady-state precondition because the run is truncated is excluded and recorded.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Reproducibility plan (F2)\n\n### 8.1 Exact flags (per run, Megatron-LM-style surface)\n\nCommon to all cells:\n```\n--pipeline-model-parallel-size <P>\n--tensor-model-parallel-size 1\n--num-layers <L_per_stage = L_total / P>\n--hidden-size H --num-attention-heads A --seq-length S --max-position-embeddings S\n--micro-batch-size m\n--global-batch-size (P * M * m)\n--train-iters (2P + 1 + N)\n--seed 0              # fixed seed for the run; reseeded only by run index\n--recompute-activations false\n--profile            # if the build supports it; otherwise only NVTX\n```\n\nSchedule-specific:\n```\n# 1F1B\n(no extra flag)\n\n# Interleaved 1F1B, v ∈ {1, 2}\n--interleaved-1f1b\n--num-virtual-stages-per-rank v\n\n# ZB-H1\n--zero-bubble-schedule h1\n--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward <P>\n\n# ZB-2p\n--zero-bubble-schedule 2p\n--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward (2 * <P>)\n```\n\nThe names `--zero-bubble-schedule`, `--interleaved-1f1b`, `--num-virtual-stages-per-rank` are the conventional names in the Megatron-LM ZB integration; if the concrete codebase uses different names, that is a Section 9 \"open mapping\" item, not a change in design.\n\n### 8.2 Cell list\n\n5 schedule families × 9 cells = 45 cells. For each cell:\n- `id = \"{schedule}_P{P}_M{M}_run{r}\"` where `r ∈ {1..N}`, `N ≥ 5`.\n- All cells are scheduled in randomized order across the available compute budget; the random seed for ordering is recorded in the run log.\n\n### 8.3 Data recorded per cell\n\nA single artifact per (cell, run), stored as JSON with fixed schema:\n- `meta`: `P, M, schedule, v (if applicable), num_layers, hidden, heads, seq_len, micro_batch, model_sha, framework_sha, gpu_model, gpu_count, interconnect_topology, cuda_version, nccl_version, run_id, timestamp, rand_seed, profiler_floor_us`.\n- `iterations`: list of length `T = 2P + 1 + N`, indexed by `t ∈ [0, T-1]`. Each entry has:\n  - `kept` (bool) — `true` iff `t ∈ [2P, T-2]`.\n  - `iter_t_iter_us` (int) — wall-clock iteration time on rank 0 of the pipeline (other ranks redundant for `t_iter`, recorded for variance).\n  - `precondition_pass` (bool) — checked on `[2P, T-2]` only.\n  - `scheduler`: per-stage array of `{stage, exec_set_empty_count, microsteps_completed}`.\n  - `events`: per-rank (only rank 0 of each stage by default; if comm asymmetry is suspected, all ranks) array of `{stage, op, mb_idx, t_dispatch_us, t_complete_us, is_comm: bool, comm_peer: int?, comm_bytes: int?}` for every μ-step.\n- `summary` (computed only over kept iterations):\n  - `B_ideal` (float in [0, 1]).\n  - `B_wall` (float in [0, 1]).\n  - `rho_comm` (float in [0, 1]).\n  - `iteration_time_us` (median + IQR over kept iterations).\n  - `pass_a_ideal` (bool), `pass_b_wall` (bool), `decision` ∈ {`pass`, `fail_a`, `fail_b`, `unresolved_floor`, `excluded_diagnostic`, `not_run`}.\n\nAcross N ≥ 5 runs:\n- `agg`: `{B_ideal: median, B_ideal_IQR, B_wall: median, B_wall_IQR, rho_comm: median}` over the N runs.\n\n### 8.4 Analysis script plan\n\nPseudocode for the analysis pipeline; this is the design's analysis contract, not an implementation claim.\n\n```\nfor cell in cells:\n  runs = load(cell.json * N)\n  # 1. Warmup / tail drop and steady-state check\n  kept = [r for r in runs if r.precondition_pass]\n  if len(kept) < N: mark cell as 'excluded_diagnostic'\n\n  # 2. Scheduler-ideal\n  B_ideal_per_run = []\n  for r in kept:\n    numer = sum(s.exec_set_empty_count for s in r.scheduler)\n    denom = r.meta.P * r.meta.M\n    B_ideal_per_run.append(numer / denom)\n  B_ideal = median(B_ideal_per_run)\n  pass_a_ideal = (B_ideal == 0)   # exact zero, per U7a\n\n  # 3. Wall-clock\n  B_wall_per_run = []\n  rho_comm_per_run = []\n  for r in kept:\n    T_iter = r.kept.iter_t_iter_us\n    T_busy = max(stage_union_busy(r.kept.events) over stages)\n    T_comm, rho = comm_overlap_metric(r.kept.events)\n    B_wall_per_run.append(1.0 - (T_busy - T_comm * rho) / T_iter)\n    rho_comm_per_run.append(rho)\n  B_wall = median(B_wall_per_run)\n  rho_comm = median(rho_comm_per_run)\n\n  # 4. Profile floor\n  f = r.meta.profiler_floor_us\n  resolution = (r.meta.P * r.meta.M * f) / T_iter\n  eps = max(resolution, 0.005)   # default stated epsilon\n  pass_b_wall = (B_wall <= eps)\n\n  # 5. Pass / fail / unresolved\n  decision = (\n    'pass'                    if pass_a_ideal and pass_b_wall\n    else 'fail_a_ideal'       if not pass_a_ideal\n    else 'fail_b_wall'        if not pass_b_wall\n    else 'unresolved'\n  )\n  cell.summary = {B_ideal, B_wall, rho_comm, iteration_time_us, decision}\n\n# Cross-cell comparisons\nprimary_pairs = [(ZB2p, 1F1B), (ZB2p, ZB-H1)] for each (P, M)\nsecondary_pairs = [(ZB2p, Interleaved v=2)] for each (P, M)\nreport per (P, M): B_ideal_diff = medians, B_wall_diff, rho_comm_diff\n```\n\nOutput artifacts:\n- `cells.csv` — one row per cell with the summary numbers.\n- `candidates_for_claim_per_cell.csv` — one row per `(P, M)` cell with decision for ZB-2p and per-comparison effect size.\n- `diagnostics/{cell_id}/iteration_timeline.json` — per-iteration intervals for diagnostic mode cells.\n\n### 8.5 Numerical reporting rules (to keep results unambiguous)\n\n- `B_ideal` reported exactly as the rational `Σ empty / (P · M)`; if non-zero by any amount, the ZB-2p claim is falsified for that cell under (a).\n- `B_wall` reported with a paired `resolution` column. A ZB-2p cell with `B_wall = resolution + δ` is **not** a pass even if the absolute number looks small; `δ` is the part that is *over* the floor.\n- Per-cell median (not mean) is the headline; the IQR is the dispersion; cells whose IQR exceeds their median are not used as evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Open mapping notes (do not re-open unless contradicted by source)\n\nThese are points where the *concrete codebase* may differ from the Megatron-LM-style surface assumed in this design. Listed so they can be reconciled without redesign.\n\n- The exact CLI flag names `--zero-bubble-schedule`, `--interleaved-1f1b`, `--num-virtual-stages-per-rank` may be spelled differently in the actual codebase; the configuration *intent* is what binds.\n- The expected value `B_ideal(1F1B) = (P − 1) / (M + P − 1)` is the standard textbook 1F1B result; if the codebase's 1F1B has customization (e.g., a warmup variant or a per-stage shaping), the sanity-check target value is the *actual* baseline the codebase produces on this (P, M), not the textbook value.\n- If `--recompute-activations` is forced on by memory, Section 7.3 applies and the ZB-recompute-aware variant of the integration must be cited; otherwise the cell's `B_wall` is reported as `unresolved_recompute`.\n- The Interleaved 1F1B / Vanilla 1F1B equivalence at `v = 1` is checked instrument-side, not enforced by the design.\n\n---\n\n## 10. What this design does *not* claim\n\n- It does not claim an experiment was run. No timing trace, no bubble fraction, and no per-cell decision is reported in this document.\n- It does not claim a specific profiler floor in microseconds; the floor `f` is a measurement to be made on the target hardware per Section 5.3 before the design is considered live.\n- It does not claim a specific GPU / interconnect / framework version; per-cell metadata captures the actual values at run time.\n- It does not claim ZB-2p's claim is true or false; the design's job is to test it with stated instruments and stated pass criteria, and the result is whatever the kept iterations show after the kept-iterations policy is applied.", "evidence": "- `/workspace/task.json.documents.track.md` literal text: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- File-system inspection: `find /workspace -type f` returned exactly `/workspace/task.json` (verified twice).\n- `nvidia-smi` binary present at `/usr/bin/nvidia-smi` but fails with \"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\", matching `environment.gpu_enabled: false`.\n- `pip3 list` and Python check: Python 3.12.3, no `torch` / `deepspeed` / `apex` / `transformers`. 24 logical CPUs. `network: disabled`.\n- ZB-2p scheduler properties (M/P ratio cells, M needed for steady state, W/F overlap, ZB-H1 vs. ZB-2p difference) are tied to the publicly known \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\" paper (Qi, Yi, You, ICLR 2024) and the Megatron-LM-style ZB integration; treated as the canonical source, to be reconciled against the specific codebase SHA at run time per Section 9.\n- Locked decisions U1–U9 + F1, F2 from the researcher's approval message are used as binding inputs; defaults from the prior plan are used for any sub-item not in U1–U9.\n- No measurements were executed; all numeric outputs in the design (cell counts, N, T, ε bounds) are design parameters, not observations.", "assumptions": ["A1. The target codebase exposes the Megatron-LM-style ZB integration; the rule --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 * P selects ZB-2p, P selects ZB-H1, exactly as the user locked in U1 and U2. If the actual flag spelling differs, Section 9 marks the mapping as a to-do, not a re-design.", "A2. \"Near-zero bubble\" is a steady-state property; warmup / cooldown bubbles are explicitly excluded by the kept-iterations policy (U4) and the 2P+1 discard window is sufficient to enter steady state for every (P, M/P) cell in the grid (U2).", "A3. The multi-GPU pool used to run the design is uniform (same per-peer interconnect between every stage pair); heterogeneous interconnect is treated as a disqualifier (Section 7.2) and not as a confounding factor.", "A4. Recompute is off for the primary pass criterion (Section 7.3); the design reports a separate robustness check with recompute on rather than blending it into pass/fail.", "A5. Optimizer / weight-update is overlapped with the next iteration's F in the codebase's ZB-2p path; if it is not, the codebase is disqualified for the wall-clock metric and the failure is reported as a codebase-level finding, not an algorithm finding (Section 7.4).", "A6. Communication is treated as overlap with compute per U6; comm time is reported separately with overlap ratio rho_comm and is not folded into the bubble fraction.", "A7. The profiler floor f is measured per Section 5.3 before the design goes live; the design document itself does not pre-commit to a numeric value of f.", "A8. Replication is N >= 5 timed iterations per cell, with cell order randomized across runs, per U8.", "A9. The pass criteria in Section 6 are ties to the user's locked decision U7 (scheduler-ideal = 0 by construction; wall-clock <= floor) and are stated in advance of any run."], "uncertainties": ["U1 (resolved as locked). Codebase: Megatron-LM-style ZB integration; both scheduler event log and CUDA-event/NVTX timeline are assumed available.", "U2 (resolved as locked). Model: GPT-like Transformer; cell grid P in {2, 4, 8} x M/P in {2, 4, 8}.", "U3 (resolved as locked). Both metrics (scheduler-ideal and wall-clock) are computed per cell and the gap is reported.", "U4 (resolved as locked). First 2P iterations and the last 1 iteration are discarded; a busy-interval MAD precondition gates including a cell.", "U5 (resolved as locked). 1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B v in {1, 2}, ZB-H1, ZB-2p. Primary comparisons ZB-2p vs. 1F1B and ZB-2p vs. ZB-H1; secondary vs. Interleaved v=2.", "U6 (resolved as locked). P2P / NCCL is not charged to bubble; comm is reported separately with an overlap ratio.", "U7 (resolved as locked). Pass requires scheduler-ideal = 0 exactly and wall-clock <= empirical floor + epsilon.", "U8 (resolved as locked). N >= 5 repeats; median + IQR; randomized cell order.", "U9 (resolved as locked). CUDA events and NVTX are the profiler; the absolute microseconds-per-microstep floor is declared in advance via a synthetic null-loop measurement per Section 5.3.", "F1 (resolved). Feasibility and risk section including load imbalance, heterogeneous interconnect, recompute, optimizer overlap, memory pressure, profiler overhead, and feasibility — with stated mitigations and disqualifiers for each.", "F2 (resolved). Reproducibility section including exact flag list, cell list, per-cell data records, and the analysis script pseudocode that maps the records into the two bubble-fraction estimates.", "Remaining item not resolvable from workspace alone: the specific framework git SHA, GPU model, interconnect topology, NCCL version, and CUDA driver at run time — these are recorded as per-cell metadata rather than fixed a priori."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-30601296-5432-4d2e-bea5-26094ebead6c", "tool_call": {"id": "call-30601296-5432-4d2e-bea5-26094ebead6c", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Submit final ZB-2p bubble-rate experimental design\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experimental Design: Does ZB-2p Achieve Near-Zero Bubble Rate on Multi-GPU?\\n\\nDesign-only document. Source-supported setup from the user's locked decisions (U1\\u2013U9 + F1, F2) is used directly. Where the design hinges on facts about the ZB-2p scheduler itself, those facts are tied to the publicly known \\\"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\\\" paper (Qi, Yi, You, ICLR 2024) and its reference Megatron-LM-style integration; they are treated as the working source of truth until the specific codebase SHA is fixed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Scope and question\\n\\nTest, on a multi-GPU pool, whether the schedule emitted by the ZB-2p scheduler (Megatron-LM-style flag surface, enabled by `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 \\u00d7 num_pipeline_stages`) has a measured bubble fraction close to zero when run under steady-state conditions on a GPT-like Transformer block.\\n\\nThe design produces two numeric estimates per `(P, M, schedule)` cell: a **scheduler-ideal** bubble fraction and a **wall-clock** bubble fraction. The pass rule (Section 6) requires both to fall under stated thresholds.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Locked-design summary (binding from the researcher)\\n\\n| ID | Decision | Adopted as |\\n|----|----------|-----------|\\n| U1 | Codebase = Megatron-LM-style ZB-2p; profiling uses both scheduler event log and CUDA-event/NVTX timeline | Both instruments are described; (a) is primary for scheduler-ideal metric, (b) is primary for wall-clock metric |\\n| U2 | Model = GPT-like Transformer; cells cover P \\u2208 {2, 4, 8} \\u00d7 M/P \\u2208 {2, 4, 8} | Full 3 \\u00d7 3 cell grid per schedule |\\n| U3 | Two definitions are computed per cell: scheduler-ideal and wall-clock | Both computed; gap is reported and reasoned about |\\n| U4 | Discard first 2 \\u00d7 P iterations and the final iteration; per-stage busy-interval variance is checked before the kept window | Strict inclusion rule; steady-state sanity check is a precondition, not a result |\\n| U5 | Schedules: 1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B v \\u2208 {1, 2}, ZB-H1, ZB-2p | Four schedule families; v is a within-Interleaved-1F1B factor, not a cross-schedule factor |\\n| U6 | P2P/NCCL is not charged to bubble; comm is reported separately with an overlap ratio | Wall-clock bubble = idle time not attributable to compute or measured comm |\\n| U7 | Pass criteria: (a) scheduler-ideal = 0 by construction and verifiable from the log; (b) wall-clock bubble \\u2264 measured comm/compute floor + \\u03b5 | Pre-stated; failure on either criterion fails the cell |\\n| U8 | N \\u2265 5 timed iterations per cell; report median + IQR; randomized cell order | Fixed before any launch |\\n| U9 | CUDA events + NVTX allowed; absolute \\u03bcs floor per micro-step is declared in advance | Floor is measured empirically on the target hardware before the design is considered live |\\n\\nSection 7 (F1) lists feasiblity / risk and the disqualifiers each implies.\\nSection 8 (F2) is the reproducibility plan (flags, cell list, data records, analysis script).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Definitions used in this document\\n\\n- **P**: pipeline-model-parallel size (number of stages).\\n- **M**: number of micro-batches per pipeline (i.e., `gradient_accumulation_steps` set such that global_batch = `P \\u00d7 M \\u00d7 micro_batch_size`).\\n- **v**: Interleaved 1F1B virtual-stage factor. v = 1 collapses to a vanilla 1F1B layout (no stage-chunking) and is reported alongside the explicit 1F1B cell to make the equivalence checkable.\\n- **\\u03bc-step**: one ranked, type-tagged operation (`F`, `B`, `W`, `recv`, `send`) emitted by the scheduler for one (stage, micro-batch).\\n- **Steady-state window**: per cell, iterations `[2P, T-1]` where T is the last iteration (so first 2P are discarded and the final iteration is discarded).\\n- **Scheduler-ideal bubble fraction** `B_ideal`: per iteration, per stage, the number of \\u03bc-steps where the scheduler's executable-set was empty, summed over all stages, divided by `P \\u00d7 M`.\\n- **Wall-clock bubble fraction** `B_wall`: `1 \\u2212 (T_busy \\u2212 T_comm,overlapped) / T_iter`, where `T_busy` is the maximal busy interval across stages (computed as the union of CUDA-event-marked compute intervals on that rank), `T_comm,overlapped` is the sub-interval of P2P / NCCL time that overlaps with compute on the same rank, and `T_iter` is the per-rank iteration wall time.\\n- **Overlap ratio** `\\u03c1_comm`: `T_comm,overlapped / T_comm` per rank; reported separately, not folded into `B_wall`.\\n\\nThe two metrics are linked by:\\n`B_wall \\u2265 B_ideal + \\u03b5(floor)`\\nwhere `\\u03b5(floor)` is the gap attributable to whatever the scheduler accounts for as \\\"busy\\\" but the CUDA-event view sees as idle (e.g., plus small synchronization margins below the profiler floor); below the profiler floor the gap is unmeasurable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Cell design\\n\\n### 4.1 Cell grid\\n\\nFor each schedule in `{1F1B, Interleaved v=1, Interleaved v=2, ZB-H1, ZB-2p}`, the cell grid is:\\n\\n| | M/P = 2 | M/P = 4 | M/P = 8 |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| P = 2 | M = 4 | M = 8 | M = 16 |\\n| P = 4 | M = 8 | M = 16 | M = 32 |\\n| P = 8 | M = 16 | M = 32 | M = 64 |\\n\\nCell count: 5 schedule families \\u00d7 9 (P, M) cells = **45 cells**. Each cell is repeated N \\u2265 5 times = **\\u2265 225 timed runs** (and per run, ~2P + N iteration-level records after warmup).\\n\\n### 4.2 Primary vs. secondary comparisons (declared in advance, per U5)\\n\\n- **Primary (decision-driving):**\\n  - ZB-2p vs. 1F1B, on every (P, M) cell.\\n  - ZB-2p vs. ZB-H1, on every (P, M) cell.\\n- **Secondary (contextual):**\\n  - ZB-2p vs. Interleaved 1F1B (v = 2), on every (P, M) cell.\\n  - Interleaved v = 1 vs. vanilla 1F1B equivalence check (sanity, not part of pass/fail).\\n\\nThe Interleaved v = 1 \\u27fa vanilla 1F1B equivalence cell acts as a control: if the two diverge by more than the floor, the cell is suspicious and excluded from the headline numbers with a recorded rationale.\\n\\n### 4.3 What \\\"warmup\\\" and \\\"steady state\\\" mean concretely\\n\\n- **Iteration budget per run**: `T = 2P + 1 + N` (the first 2P are warmup, the last 1 is dropped, the middle N are timed). For the largest cell (P = 8), `T = 22`; N = 5 timed iterations dominate, so steady state is required to hold inside those 5 iterations.\\n- **Steady-state check (precondition to keep the cell)**: for the N timed iterations, compute the per-stage busy-interval length in each iteration; the cell passes the precondition if the **median absolute deviation across the N repetitions** for every stage's median busy interval is below a per-cell threshold (default proposal: \\u2264 5% of the cell's iteration time). If this fails, the cell is re-run under the diagnostic mode (Section 6.2) before being declared failed or passed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Instrumentation plan\\n\\nTwo independent instruments, one per metric:\\n\\n### 5.1 Scheduler event log \\u2192 scheduler-ideal bubble `B_ideal`\\n\\n- **Primary hook**: the scheduler's per-stage executable-set state at the end of each \\u03bc-step. Each emitted event is `{stage, \\u03bc-step index, micro-batch index, op \\u2208 {F, B, W}, exec_set_size_at_dispatch, ts_dispatch, ts_complete}`.\\n- **Empty-set counter**: `B_ideal` is computed as\\n  `\\u03a3 over (stage, \\u03bc-step) of 1[exec_set_size_at_dispatch == 0] / (P \\u00d7 M)`\\n  inside the steady-state window.\\n- **Why primary**: this is the metric whose zero-ness the paper (and the user's locked decision U7a) defines \\\"near-zero\\\" by. If this is non-zero for a ZB-2p cell, ZB-2p's claim is falsified for that cell regardless of wall-clock.\\n- **Source assumption (paper-anchored)**: ZB-2p's schedule is constructed so that, in any iteration with the schedule in its periodic regime, every stage has at least one executable \\u03bc-step. The schedulability invariant is therefore checkable from the log.\\n\\n### 5.2 CUDA-event + NVTX timeline \\u2192 wall-clock bubble `B_wall` and overlap `\\u03c1_comm`\\n\\n- **Primary hook**: per-rank `cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)` pairs bracketing every emitted \\u03bc-step; NVTX ranges named with the same `{stage, \\u03bc-step, op}` tags so the CUDA events can be reconciled with the scheduler log.\\n- **Boundary sync**: one `cuda.synchronize()` at the start of iteration and one at the end of iteration; intra-iteration timing uses event pairs without a global sync, so \\u03bc-step resolution is not gated by ring-buffer depth.\\n- **Compute intervals**: begin = event-start of a `F/B/W` \\u03bc-step dispatch; end = event-complete of the same op. Multiple ops on the same stage may chain into a single contiguous interval if they share a CUDA stream and there is no intervening host-side wait \\u2014 the merge rule is precise (no overlap on the same stream) and produces a noise-free union.\\n- **Communication intervals**: tagged via NVTX around `torch.distributed.send`/`recv` and around any NCCL collective boundary. Per-rank P2P / NCCL time = sum of those interval durations on that rank.\\n- **Overlap**: `T_comm,overlapped` on a rank = length of `interval_comm \\u2229 interval_busy` for that rank, summed.\\n- **Why primary for `B_wall`**: it is the only way to distinguish compute from comm from idle on the same rank, which is required by U6.\\n\\n### 5.3 Profiler floor declaration (U9)\\n\\n- **Floor measurement (design-time, not run-time)**: a synthetic per-\\u03bc-step loop of `n = 1000` event-pair.create / record / synchronize / elapsed_time sequences is run on the target hardware. The 99th-percentile `cudaEventElapsedTime` over `n` is declared as the **floor `f` in microseconds per \\u03bc-step**.\\n- **Bound on `B_wall` resolution**: a single \\u03bc-step that is \\\"actually bubbling\\\" but shorter than `f` is unprovable. Therefore `B_wall` per cell has resolution `\\u2248 P \\u00b7 M \\u00b7 f / T_iter`. This is the relevant \\u03b5 used in U7b: pass requires `B_wall \\u2264 f_bounded` where `f_bounded = clamp(resolution \\u00d7 ceiling, profiler_resolution_min, stated_eps)`. A default stated \\u03b5 for the design = max(resolution, 0.5%) per cell is used unless a tighter target is pre-set.\\n\\nThe floor `f` is recorded in the cell's metadata and reported with each cell's pass/fail decision; it is not a number being invented for this design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Pass / fail criteria (declared a priori)\\n\\n### 6.1 Pass rule per cell\\n\\nA single `(P, M, schedule)` cell **passes** iff both:\\n\\n(a) **Scheduler-ideal** `B_ideal == 0` exactly, inside the steady-state window, and this is verifiable from the scheduler event log. For ZB-2p this is expected to hold by construction; for 1F1B and Interleaved 1F1B (v = 2), the expected positive value of `B_ideal` is not what is being tested here, but the same instrument must report it consistently with theory (sanity check, see 6.3).\\n\\n(b) **Wall-clock** `B_wall \\u2264 f_bounded` where `f_bounded` is the per-cell floor bound defined in Section 5.3. Equivalently, the residual idle time that is neither compute nor accounted-for communication is below the profiler's resolution.\\n\\nThe cell **fails** if either (a) or (b) is violated within the kept iterations, after the steady-state precondition has held.\\n\\n### 6.2 Diagnostic mode (when a cell fails precondition)\\n\\nCells whose per-stage busy-interval MAD exceeds the threshold enter a diagnostic mode that:\\n1. Re-runs the cell with one-iteration resolution NVTX ranges around every \\u03bc-step and outputs the per-iteration timeline as a separate artifact (without altering the kept / dropped iteration policy).\\n2. Records the run as \\\"diagnostic\\\" in the cell's metadata and excludes its summary number from the headline table.\\n3. Does not silently change the pass criterion.\\n\\n### 6.3 Theory sanity (run once, before pass/fail is interpreted)\\n\\nRun the analyzer on 1F1B cells first; verify:\\n- `B_ideal(1F1B) \\u2248 (P - 1) / (M + P - 1)` to within `\\u00b11/(P\\u00b7M)` (paper expectation).\\n- `B_ideal(ZB-H1) \\u2248 (P - 1) / (M + P - 1) \\u00b7 (1 - some constant)` smaller than 1F1B but still positive (paper expectation).\\n- `B_ideal(Interleaved v=2) \\u2248 \\u2026` smaller still.\\n\\nIf any of these deviate by more than the per-cell resolution, the design's metric plumbing is broken and the run is treated as dataset-level failure: do not interpret ZB-2p results until the baseline instruments reconcile with theory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Feasibility and risk analysis (F1)\\n\\nEach risk below: **trigger**, **consequence for the claim**, **mitigation**, **disqualifier**.\\n\\n### 7.1 Stage load imbalance\\n- **Trigger**: per-stage busy time on a 1F1B control cell has max/stdev ratio above a stated threshold (default proposal: max / median > 1.10).\\n- **Consequence**: ZB-2p's \\\"near zero\\\" claim is stage-symmetric by construction; if stages are not balanced in floating-point or activation memory, the bubble is dominated by imbalance, not by scheduling.\\n- **Mitigation**: equal per-stage layer counts; vocabulary and sequence-length-specific stages (input embed, loss head) moved to a separate \\\"edge\\\" rank outside the pipeline timing window, or sized to match the body stage.\\n- **Disqualifier**: any cell where stage imbalance exceeds the threshold AND the imbalance is correlated with N \\u2260 stable \\u2192 drop the cell and record.\\n\\n### 7.2 Heterogeneous interconnect\\n- **Trigger**: stages span nodes with non-uniform bandwidth (e.g., partial intra-rail vs cross-rail), or NVLink absent in some peer pairs.\\n- **Consequence**: per-stage communication time differs; the wall-clock bubble becomes communication-bound, not scheduling-bound. ZB-2p's claim is about *scheduling* bubble, not comm.\\n- **Mitigation**: restrict runs to a single fully-connected GPU island (single node, same NVLink/NVSwitch fabric between every peer pair). All stages within the same physical node.\\n- **Disqualifier**: if a multi-node setup is the only option, the DDR / IB fabric differs between stage pairs \\u2192 ZB-2p cells cannot be passed on `B_wall` in that environment; only `B_ideal` is reported, with a noted caveat that `B_wall` cannot be bounded.\\n\\n### 7.3 Activation recompute interaction\\n- **Trigger**: `--recompute-activations` enabled (full or selective).\\n- **Consequence**: recompute blends with the B phase; ZB-2p's split (F-before-attn / F-after-attn, B / W) is benchmarked on the *original* partition in the public reference; if recompute reorders what is sent between stages, the schedule's prefetch / overlap assumption is broken.\\n- **Mitigation**: keep `--recompute-activations false` (or its ZB-aware variant, if the codebase exposes one) for the primary pass criterion. Report a separate robustness check with compute-on rather than mixing into pass/fail.\\n- **Disqualifier**: if the target workload's memory budget forces recompute always-on, the design's primary pass criterion cannot be evaluated without the codebase's ZB-recompute handling, which must then be cited explicitly.\\n\\n### 7.4 Optimizer-step / weight-update overlap policy\\n- **Trigger**: chosen codebase's ZB-2p path may or may not overlap `W` with the next iteration's `F`; this is implementation policy, not algorithm definition.\\n- **Consequence**: if W is forced to be synchronous and to precede the next F, ZB-2p's \\\"near zero\\\" claim is partially defeated: the residual is now sized by `T_W / T_iter`, not by 0.\\n- **Mitigation**: confirm in the target codebase that the ZB-2p schedule allows `W` to overlap with the next iteration's `F`; if not, this is a *codebase* bug or feature gap, not an algorithm failure, and is a documented disqualifier for that codebase.\\n- **Disqualifier**: any codebase where W is serialized before the next F cannot pass the (a) zeroness claim for the wall-clock metric, only the scheduler-ideal metric.\\n\\n### 7.5 Memory pressure / allocator stalls\\n- **Trigger**: an activation memory budget per stage that approaches the device's available memory, especially with large M.\\n- **Consequence**: allocator-induced stalls are neither F, B, W, nor communication, so they fall into the residual (U6 \\\"neither compute nor accounted-for communication\\\"). If large enough, they push `B_wall` above the floor and the cell *looks* like bubble when in fact it is memory pressure.\\n- **Mitigation**: precompute per-stage activation-memory budget `cost(P, M, model_shape)` and a `safety_margin`; reject any cell whose peak budget + optimizer state + gradient + activations exceeds device memory by less than 25 % headroom.\\n- **Disqualifier**: any cell whose runtime OOM or near-OOM event is detected (logged as allocator warnings or `cudaGetLastError` nonzero) is dropped from the headline numbers.\\n\\n### 7.6 Profiler injection overhead\\n- **Trigger**: NVTX wrapping every \\u03bc-step changes the steady-state timing.\\n- **Consequence**: the *instrumented* run is the run whose bubble is being measured; injecting a perturbing instrument is a delta-t measurement not a true measurement.\\n- **Mitigation**: run a non-instrumented baseline once per cell to compute `T_iter` for the denominator of `B_wall`, and run instrumented runs only for the per-\\u03bc-step interval breakdown. The bubble fraction's denominator is the non-instrumented iteration time.\\n- **Disqualifier**: if the delta between instrumented and non-instrumented `T_iter` exceeds the profiler floor `f`, the cell's `B_wall` is reported as `unresolved` and excluded from the pass / fail rule.\\n\\n### 7.7 Cell count feasibility\\n- **Trigger**: 45 cells \\u00d7 N \\u2265 5 timed iterations \\u00d7 (2P + N) iterations per run.\\n- **Consequence**: feasible on a multi-GPU pool if each cell fits inside ~1 GPU-hour. The largest cell (P = 8, M/P = 8) is the load-bearing run and the one most likely to be constrained by allocator, not by time.\\n- **Mitigation**: randomize cell execution order across the available time budget; if a cell fails because the budget is exhausted, it is recorded as `not run` and not as `failed`.\\n- **Disqualifier**: a cell whose run concludes without passing the steady-state precondition because the run is truncated is excluded and recorded.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Reproducibility plan (F2)\\n\\n### 8.1 Exact flags (per run, Megatron-LM-style surface)\\n\\nCommon to all cells:\\n```\\n--pipeline-model-parallel-size <P>\\n--tensor-model-parallel-size 1\\n--num-layers <L_per_stage = L_total / P>\\n--hidden-size H --num-attention-heads A --seq-length S --max-position-embeddings S\\n--micro-batch-size m\\n--global-batch-size (P * M * m)\\n--train-iters (2P + 1 + N)\\n--seed 0              # fixed seed for the run; reseeded only by run index\\n--recompute-activations false\\n--profile            # if the build supports it; otherwise only NVTX\\n```\\n\\nSchedule-specific:\\n```\\n# 1F1B\\n(no extra flag)\\n\\n# Interleaved 1F1B, v \\u2208 {1, 2}\\n--interleaved-1f1b\\n--num-virtual-stages-per-rank v\\n\\n# ZB-H1\\n--zero-bubble-schedule h1\\n--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward <P>\\n\\n# ZB-2p\\n--zero-bubble-schedule 2p\\n--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward (2 * <P>)\\n```\\n\\nThe names `--zero-bubble-schedule`, `--interleaved-1f1b`, `--num-virtual-stages-per-rank` are the conventional names in the Megatron-LM ZB integration; if the concrete codebase uses different names, that is a Section 9 \\\"open mapping\\\" item, not a change in design.\\n\\n### 8.2 Cell list\\n\\n5 schedule families \\u00d7 9 cells = 45 cells. For each cell:\\n- `id = \\\"{schedule}_P{P}_M{M}_run{r}\\\"` where `r \\u2208 {1..N}`, `N \\u2265 5`.\\n- All cells are scheduled in randomized order across the available compute budget; the random seed for ordering is recorded in the run log.\\n\\n### 8.3 Data recorded per cell\\n\\nA single artifact per (cell, run), stored as JSON with fixed schema:\\n- `meta`: `P, M, schedule, v (if applicable), num_layers, hidden, heads, seq_len, micro_batch, model_sha, framework_sha, gpu_model, gpu_count, interconnect_topology, cuda_version, nccl_version, run_id, timestamp, rand_seed, profiler_floor_us`.\\n- `iterations`: list of length `T = 2P + 1 + N`, indexed by `t \\u2208 [0, T-1]`. Each entry has:\\n  - `kept` (bool) \\u2014 `true` iff `t \\u2208 [2P, T-2]`.\\n  - `iter_t_iter_us` (int) \\u2014 wall-clock iteration time on rank 0 of the pipeline (other ranks redundant for `t_iter`, recorded for variance).\\n  - `precondition_pass` (bool) \\u2014 checked on `[2P, T-2]` only.\\n  - `scheduler`: per-stage array of `{stage, exec_set_empty_count, microsteps_completed}`.\\n  - `events`: per-rank (only rank 0 of each stage by default; if comm asymmetry is suspected, all ranks) array of `{stage, op, mb_idx, t_dispatch_us, t_complete_us, is_comm: bool, comm_peer: int?, comm_bytes: int?}` for every \\u03bc-step.\\n- `summary` (computed only over kept iterations):\\n  - `B_ideal` (float in [0, 1]).\\n  - `B_wall` (float in [0, 1]).\\n  - `rho_comm` (float in [0, 1]).\\n  - `iteration_time_us` (median + IQR over kept iterations).\\n  - `pass_a_ideal` (bool), `pass_b_wall` (bool), `decision` \\u2208 {`pass`, `fail_a`, `fail_b`, `unresolved_floor`, `excluded_diagnostic`, `not_run`}.\\n\\nAcross N \\u2265 5 runs:\\n- `agg`: `{B_ideal: median, B_ideal_IQR, B_wall: median, B_wall_IQR, rho_comm: median}` over the N runs.\\n\\n### 8.4 Analysis script plan\\n\\nPseudocode for the analysis pipeline; this is the design's analysis contract, not an implementation claim.\\n\\n```\\nfor cell in cells:\\n  runs = load(cell.json * N)\\n  # 1. Warmup / tail drop and steady-state check\\n  kept = [r for r in runs if r.precondition_pass]\\n  if len(kept) < N: mark cell as 'excluded_diagnostic'\\n\\n  # 2. Scheduler-ideal\\n  B_ideal_per_run = []\\n  for r in kept:\\n    numer = sum(s.exec_set_empty_count for s in r.scheduler)\\n    denom = r.meta.P * r.meta.M\\n    B_ideal_per_run.append(numer / denom)\\n  B_ideal = median(B_ideal_per_run)\\n  pass_a_ideal = (B_ideal == 0)   # exact zero, per U7a\\n\\n  # 3. Wall-clock\\n  B_wall_per_run = []\\n  rho_comm_per_run = []\\n  for r in kept:\\n    T_iter = r.kept.iter_t_iter_us\\n    T_busy = max(stage_union_busy(r.kept.events) over stages)\\n    T_comm, rho = comm_overlap_metric(r.kept.events)\\n    B_wall_per_run.append(1.0 - (T_busy - T_comm * rho) / T_iter)\\n    rho_comm_per_run.append(rho)\\n  B_wall = median(B_wall_per_run)\\n  rho_comm = median(rho_comm_per_run)\\n\\n  # 4. Profile floor\\n  f = r.meta.profiler_floor_us\\n  resolution = (r.meta.P * r.meta.M * f) / T_iter\\n  eps = max(resolution, 0.005)   # default stated epsilon\\n  pass_b_wall = (B_wall <= eps)\\n\\n  # 5. Pass / fail / unresolved\\n  decision = (\\n    'pass'                    if pass_a_ideal and pass_b_wall\\n    else 'fail_a_ideal'       if not pass_a_ideal\\n    else 'fail_b_wall'        if not pass_b_wall\\n    else 'unresolved'\\n  )\\n  cell.summary = {B_ideal, B_wall, rho_comm, iteration_time_us, decision}\\n\\n# Cross-cell comparisons\\nprimary_pairs = [(ZB2p, 1F1B), (ZB2p, ZB-H1)] for each (P, M)\\nsecondary_pairs = [(ZB2p, Interleaved v=2)] for each (P, M)\\nreport per (P, M): B_ideal_diff = medians, B_wall_diff, rho_comm_diff\\n```\\n\\nOutput artifacts:\\n- `cells.csv` \\u2014 one row per cell with the summary numbers.\\n- `candidates_for_claim_per_cell.csv` \\u2014 one row per `(P, M)` cell with decision for ZB-2p and per-comparison effect size.\\n- `diagnostics/{cell_id}/iteration_timeline.json` \\u2014 per-iteration intervals for diagnostic mode cells.\\n\\n### 8.5 Numerical reporting rules (to keep results unambiguous)\\n\\n- `B_ideal` reported exactly as the rational `\\u03a3 empty / (P \\u00b7 M)`; if non-zero by any amount, the ZB-2p claim is falsified for that cell under (a).\\n- `B_wall` reported with a paired `resolution` column. A ZB-2p cell with `B_wall = resolution + \\u03b4` is **not** a pass even if the absolute number looks small; `\\u03b4` is the part that is *over* the floor.\\n- Per-cell median (not mean) is the headline; the IQR is the dispersion; cells whose IQR exceeds their median are not used as evidence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Open mapping notes (do not re-open unless contradicted by source)\\n\\nThese are points where the *concrete codebase* may differ from the Megatron-LM-style surface assumed in this design. Listed so they can be reconciled without redesign.\\n\\n- The exact CLI flag names `--zero-bubble-schedule`, `--interleaved-1f1b`, `--num-virtual-stages-per-rank` may be spelled differently in the actual codebase; the configuration *intent* is what binds.\\n- The expected value `B_ideal(1F1B) = (P \\u2212 1) / (M + P \\u2212 1)` is the standard textbook 1F1B result; if the codebase's 1F1B has customization (e.g., a warmup variant or a per-stage shaping), the sanity-check target value is the *actual* baseline the codebase produces on this (P, M), not the textbook value.\\n- If `--recompute-activations` is forced on by memory, Section 7.3 applies and the ZB-recompute-aware variant of the integration must be cited; otherwise the cell's `B_wall` is reported as `unresolved_recompute`.\\n- The Interleaved 1F1B / Vanilla 1F1B equivalence at `v = 1` is checked instrument-side, not enforced by the design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. What this design does *not* claim\\n\\n- It does not claim an experiment was run. No timing trace, no bubble fraction, and no per-cell decision is reported in this document.\\n- It does not claim a specific profiler floor in microseconds; the floor `f` is a measurement to be made on the target hardware per Section 5.3 before the design is considered live.\\n- It does not claim a specific GPU / interconnect / framework version; per-cell metadata captures the actual values at run time.\\n- It does not claim ZB-2p's claim is true or false; the design's job is to test it with stated instruments and stated pass criteria, and the result is whatever the kept iterations show after the kept-iterations policy is applied.\", \"evidence\": \"- `/workspace/task.json.documents.track.md` literal text: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- File-system inspection: `find /workspace -type f` returned exactly `/workspace/task.json` (verified twice).\\n- `nvidia-smi` binary present at `/usr/bin/nvidia-smi` but fails with \\\"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\\\", matching `environment.gpu_enabled: false`.\\n- `pip3 list` and Python check: Python 3.12.3, no `torch` / `deepspeed` / `apex` / `transformers`. 24 logical CPUs. `network: disabled`.\\n- ZB-2p scheduler properties (M/P ratio cells, M needed for steady state, W/F overlap, ZB-H1 vs. ZB-2p difference) are tied to the publicly known \\\"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\\\" paper (Qi, Yi, You, ICLR 2024) and the Megatron-LM-style ZB integration; treated as the canonical source, to be reconciled against the specific codebase SHA at run time per Section 9.\\n- Locked decisions U1\\u2013U9 + F1, F2 from the researcher's approval message are used as binding inputs; defaults from the prior plan are used for any sub-item not in U1\\u2013U9.\\n- No measurements were executed; all numeric outputs in the design (cell counts, N, T, \\u03b5 bounds) are design parameters, not observations.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. The target codebase exposes the Megatron-LM-style ZB integration; the rule --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 * P selects ZB-2p, P selects ZB-H1, exactly as the user locked in U1 and U2. If the actual flag spelling differs, Section 9 marks the mapping as a to-do, not a re-design.\", \"A2. \\\"Near-zero bubble\\\" is a steady-state property; warmup / cooldown bubbles are explicitly excluded by the kept-iterations policy (U4) and the 2P+1 discard window is sufficient to enter steady state for every (P, M/P) cell in the grid (U2).\", \"A3. The multi-GPU pool used to run the design is uniform (same per-peer interconnect between every stage pair); heterogeneous interconnect is treated as a disqualifier (Section 7.2) and not as a confounding factor.\", \"A4. Recompute is off for the primary pass criterion (Section 7.3); the design reports a separate robustness check with recompute on rather than blending it into pass/fail.\", \"A5. Optimizer / weight-update is overlapped with the next iteration's F in the codebase's ZB-2p path; if it is not, the codebase is disqualified for the wall-clock metric and the failure is reported as a codebase-level finding, not an algorithm finding (Section 7.4).\", \"A6. Communication is treated as overlap with compute per U6; comm time is reported separately with overlap ratio rho_comm and is not folded into the bubble fraction.\", \"A7. The profiler floor f is measured per Section 5.3 before the design goes live; the design document itself does not pre-commit to a numeric value of f.\", \"A8. Replication is N >= 5 timed iterations per cell, with cell order randomized across runs, per U8.\", \"A9. The pass criteria in Section 6 are ties to the user's locked decision U7 (scheduler-ideal = 0 by construction; wall-clock <= floor) and are stated in advance of any run.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1 (resolved as locked). Codebase: Megatron-LM-style ZB integration; both scheduler event log and CUDA-event/NVTX timeline are assumed available.\", \"U2 (resolved as locked). Model: GPT-like Transformer; cell grid P in {2, 4, 8} x M/P in {2, 4, 8}.\", \"U3 (resolved as locked). Both metrics (scheduler-ideal and wall-clock) are computed per cell and the gap is reported.\", \"U4 (resolved as locked). First 2P iterations and the last 1 iteration are discarded; a busy-interval MAD precondition gates including a cell.\", \"U5 (resolved as locked). 1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B v in {1, 2}, ZB-H1, ZB-2p. Primary comparisons ZB-2p vs. 1F1B and ZB-2p vs. ZB-H1; secondary vs. Interleaved v=2.\", \"U6 (resolved as locked). P2P / NCCL is not charged to bubble; comm is reported separately with an overlap ratio.\", \"U7 (resolved as locked). Pass requires scheduler-ideal = 0 exactly and wall-clock <= empirical floor + epsilon.\", \"U8 (resolved as locked). N >= 5 repeats; median + IQR; randomized cell order.\", \"U9 (resolved as locked). CUDA events and NVTX are the profiler; the absolute microseconds-per-microstep floor is declared in advance via a synthetic null-loop measurement per Section 5.3.\", \"F1 (resolved). Feasibility and risk section including load imbalance, heterogeneous interconnect, recompute, optimizer overlap, memory pressure, profiler overhead, and feasibility \\u2014 with stated mitigations and disqualifiers for each.\", \"F2 (resolved). Reproducibility section including exact flag list, cell list, per-cell data records, and the analysis script pseudocode that maps the records into the two bubble-fraction estimates.\", \"Remaining item not resolvable from workspace alone: the specific framework git SHA, GPU model, interconnect topology, NCCL version, and CUDA driver at run time \\u2014 these are recorded as per-cell metadata rather than fixed a priori.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b92742e0-5fb1-44ea-8990-f439167c64f1", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 27}
